From bb1b8029b9cc3767fac5d2e90790134db4ce0955 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dave Seddon <dave.seddon.ca@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 15 Jun 2026 09:40:02 -0700
Subject: [PATCH RFC v3 0/4] net: flow_dissector: per-shape fast-path sysctls under /proc/sys/net/flow_dissector/
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

v3 of the flow_dissector fast-path series. Supersedes v1 (three-patch
split applied via net-next as eeca3eb493b8) and v2-experiment (single
inline patch 8013aee91ccb).

What changed since v2-inline:

  - Single sysctl net.core.flow_dissector_fastpath replaced by a new
    per-shape subtree at /proc/sys/net/flow_dissector/{eth_ip, vlan,
    qinq, vxlan_inner}. Each is a separate DEFINE_STATIC_KEY_FALSE
    with its own sysctl. Operators enable only the shapes their
    deployment uses.

  - Hard rename. net.core.flow_dissector_fastpath is gone; its
    replacement is net.flow_dissector.eth_ip. The old knob only ever
    existed in net-next 7.1-rc4 so no released kernel had it.

  - New extension patches stack on the parent and ship their own
    sysctl + helper:
      * patch 2 - single VLAN (8021Q / 8021AD outer tag) - byte-
        identical with slow path
      * patch 3 - QinQ depth-2 (8021AD + 8021Q stack) - byte-identical
        with slow path; auto-toggle proc_handlers tie qinq <-> vlan
        so writing qinq=1 enables vlan and writing vlan=0 clears qinq
      * patch 4 - RFC EXPERIMENT, VXLAN inner descent. Independent
        knob. NOT byte-identical with the slow path; gated separately
        so operators who want only the byte-identical knobs don't
        inherit the behaviour change.

Why per-shape:

  Different deployments want different shapes. A data-centre operator
  running cake on VLAN-tagged switch trunks wants vlan + qinq. A k8s
  overlay operator wants vxlan_inner. An ISP running PPPoE will
  eventually want a fifth knob (not in this series). One coarse "all
  or nothing" sysctl doesn't fit.

  Each per-shape gate is a separate static_branch — the slow-path
  cost when a shape is disabled is one forward not-taken JMP in the
  dispatcher switch, same as the v1/v2 "umbrella" gate.

Base / where it applies:

  Patches are against net-next at c0aa5f13826d (the commit immediately
  before v2-inline 8013aee91ccb was applied). The previous series was
  withdrawn; this replaces it.

Patch-by-patch shape:

  Patch 1: opt-in fast-path for eth + IPv4/IPv6 + TCP/UDP. Same
  functionality as v2-inline but reorganised:
    - flow_dissect_fast_ipv4 / _ipv6 are separate static helpers (not
      inline in __skb_flow_dissect) — makes patches 2-4 add cleanly
    - forward declarations anchored at top of file (right after
      includes) so patch 2's VLAN helper can call them regardless of
      where the patch tool lands the helper body relative to the
      parent definitions on stable-kernel backports
    - DEFINE_STATIC_KEY_FALSE(flow_dissector_eth_ip_key), sysctl
      /proc/sys/net/flow_dissector/eth_ip
    - new late_initcall flow_dissector_sysctl_init in flow_dissector.c
      registers the net/flow_dissector subtree (pattern from
      net/mpls/af_mpls.c)

  Patch 2: single VLAN. Adds flow_dissect_fast_vlan helper +
  dispatcher case for ETH_P_8021Q / ETH_P_8021AD. Own sysctl
  /proc/sys/net/flow_dissector/vlan. Custom proc_handler
  proc_set_vlan_key (a thin wrapper today; patch 3 gives it the
  sibling-clear behaviour).

  Patch 3: QinQ. Adds vlan_depth parameter to flow_dissect_fast_vlan,
  outer tag -> KEY_VLAN, inner tag -> KEY_CVLAN. New sysctl
  /proc/sys/net/flow_dissector/qinq. Custom proc_set_qinq_key
  auto-enables vlan on write of 1; the updated proc_set_vlan_key
  clears qinq on write of 0. So `echo 1 > qinq` is enough to get
  both knobs wired up.

  Patch 4: VXLAN inner descent, RFC EXPERIMENT. Gated independently.
  Behaviour change called out in the commit message and in the
  vxlan_inner sysctl paragraph. Open design questions on slow-path
  symmetry and per-netns port discovery.

Measured - real workload (macro):

  Receiver-side CPU on the xdp2 testbed pair pi5-1 <-> pi5-2
  (Cortex-A76, 1 GbE genet, 4 parallel iperf3, 30 s cells):

    vlan UDP host_system  68.11% -> 63.66%  (-6.5%)  sysctl 0 -> 1
    qinq UDP host_system  75.87% -> 70.98%  (-6.4%)  sysctl 0 -> 1
    vxlan UDP             ~zero net (outer fast-path saving offset by
                                     inner-descent work, as designed)

  These cells are wire-saturated, so the dissector saving shows up
  as receiver CPU headroom, not throughput. On the faster links the
  per-cell macro delta sits within run-to-run noise (within ~2 pp at
  single-30 s-cell granularity on the 10/25 GbE x86 pairs) - there
  the link, not the dissector, is the bottleneck, and longer DUR +
  repeats are needed to lift the signal above x86 variance. Macro
  tables across the fleet (hp1-hp3 25 GbE mlx5 Zen 1; hp2-hp5 10 GbE
  i40e Zen 1; l-l2 25 GbE Zen 2; pi5 1 GbE Cortex-A76; bpi-f3 1 GbE
  RISC-V) live under perf-results/ in the xdp2 repo.

Measured - dissector cost in isolation (microbench):

  To show where the saving comes from, independent of NIC / qdisc /
  scheduler, a userspace libflowdis A/B (identical dissector source,
  fast-path .so vs baseline .so, synthetic eth+IPv4+TCP, 10 x 10 M
  iterations, taskset-pinned) was run on every uarch in the fleet:

    uarch                          baseline  patched  speedup
    x86 Zen 2  (TR PRO 3945WX)       12.44     6.56    -47.3%
    x86 Skylake (i9-10885H)          10.61     5.62    -47.0%
    ARM Cortex-A53 (pi3-1)          118.97    61.27    -48.5%
    ARM Cortex-A72 (pi4-1)           42.37    19.05    -55.0%
    ARM Cortex-A76 (pi5-2)           19.37     9.25    -52.3%
    RISC-V SpacemiT X60 (bpi-f3)     98.16    49.27    -49.8%
                                              (ns/pkt, lower is better)

  ~47-55% reduction in the dissector cost itself across AMD/Intel
  x86, three ARM uarches, and RISC-V - i.e. the fast-path generalises
  across ISAs (the static_branch gate resolves through each arch's
  jump_label implementation). One honest exception: x86 Zen 1
  (Ryzen 5 PRO 2400G) shows ~0% at p50 - the saving is masked on that
  older uarch (documented in the xdp2 perf-results). Default-off cost
  remains one not-taken JMP per dissector call on every arch.

Open items not addressed in this RFC:

  - Selftests at tools/testing/selftests/net/ comparing fast-path
    output byte-for-byte to slow-path output across the eligible
    shapes.
  - PPPoE, MPLS, GRE shape extensions (candidate table in
    packet-flow-context.md §10 in the xdp2 repo).
  - VXLAN port per-netns discovery (still hardcoded IANA 4789).
  - slow-path UDP-encap descent symmetry for patch 4.

